And by Allied Signal.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:00:59.970
A technology leader.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.080
in aerospace, electronics, automotive products, and engineered materials.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:49.450
any measure.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.350
The plants of the tropical rainforest are beautiful,

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:53.550
and mysterious.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.000
In this profusion,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.920
of leaves and blooms are thousands of plant species.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:04.850
many still undiscovered.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:05.690
and unnamed.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.040
plants that provide shelter and nourishment for the animals that live among them.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.330
Plants that help sustain the Earth's atmosphere.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.090
Renewing the oxygen that makes life possible.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.840
and plants that provide

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:30.360
medicine.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:35.190
Indeed, the history of medicine has been largely the story of plants.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.530
and the potent chemicals they produce.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.300
Around the world, traditional healers using plant medications

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:50.530
provide health care to 80% of the human population.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.500
Over 4 billion people.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.840
Ayurveda, also called the science of life,

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.000
Developed in India more than 3,000

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:00.800
thousand years ago.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.180
laying the foundations for one of history's most remarkable medical traditions.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:17.410
This system of herbal remedies and natural healing is still dominant in India.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:25.840
There are more than 8,000 prescriptions in this ancient system of medicine.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.950
And factories like this one process raw materials into preparation.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.420
used by over half a billion people.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:38.460
From Ayurveda, the Western world learned about Raulphia Serpentina.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:39.760
or the snake root plant.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.710
Also known as ricerpine, it is used to control hypertension.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.010
and is a compound essential to modern cardiology.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:55.440
Like India,

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:59.440
China is the home of an ancient and sophisticated natural medicine system.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:12.630
Over 4,000 years, the Chinese have discovered, developed and refined more than 11,000 medical prescriptions.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.010
based on the natural chemistry of plants.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:23.890
These mixtures and compounds were based on centuries of empirical observation of their effects on the human body.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.980
Using ancient recipes, remedies are carefully weighed.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.580
and measured in accordance with the physician's requests.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.380
While the final prescription may contain unusual looking elements,

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.010
The herbal drugstore is as common in China

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.920
as the corner drugstore in the West.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.880
Even in parts of the world where vegetation is sparse,

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:57.000
There is a legacy of medicinal plants.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:09.540
For centuries, the Bedouin have used Ami seeds.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:10.890
which contain chromelin.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:12.570
as an effective asthma remedy.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.730
even our own Western pharmacology.

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:28.190
with its pills and white crystalline powders.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:29.930
owes much of its potency.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:41.500
to the plant kingdom. Many common medications were first derived from plants. More than 2400

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.970
years ago, the Greeks used extracts of willow bark to treat the disease.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.160
The active compound it contained is better known today as aspirin.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:58.190
The flowering foxglove was used to treat heart problems as far back as the 15th century.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:05.200
Today, Digitalis from the Foxglove is still an important component of heart medications.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.680
Aspirin, Digitalis, and many other medicines are now synthetics.

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:13.950
chemical versions of plant compounds.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.400
But not all modern medicines can be synthesized.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:24.190
Over 25% of the drugs prescribed in the U.S. still contain plant materials as their principal active ingredient.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:29.100
According to a recent study by a Congressional

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:29.740
group.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:31.500
The U.S. imported...

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.760
over 24 million dollars a year of medicinal plants in 1974 and then turn these into three

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:40.260
billion dollars worth of medicines so it is fairly a significant business

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:47.060
What is it about plants that gives them the power to heal?

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:48.160
or to harm.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:51.030
for in addition to their beneficial uses,

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:55.430
Some plants are well known to contain chemicals that are harmful to the human body.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:00.000
Rhubarb leaves, for instance, produce a deadly...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.920
of chemicals that can cause diarrhea,

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:03.040
Vomiting.

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:05.090
and can prevent the blood from clotting.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:13.330
Every part of the potato plant, except the fleshy tuber,

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:14.980
can cause severe poisoning.

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:19.570
What lies behind the mysterious chemistry of plants?

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:29.300
Of all living things, only plants have the power to transform light energy into life energy.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.970
This precise chemical process is known as photosynthesis.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:41.870
These leaf cells under the microscope

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:45.920
reveal a constant stream of green particles called chloroplasts.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.960
Magnified 40,000 times, the inside of the chloroplast is seen to be made up of tiny light collectors

00:07:58.250 --> 00:07:59.210
arranged in layers.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:06.980
There are thousands of such chloroplasts in each green leaf, performing the task of photosynthesis.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:13.540
Within the leaf, carbon dioxide absorbed from the atmosphere and water taken up by the root system

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:18.120
undergo complex chemical reactions fueled by energy from sunlight.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:23.400
The result is the simple sugar glucose, which is stored in the leaf cells as food.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:29.960
The oxygen that is released back into the atmosphere...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.240
is simply a by-product of the process.

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:38.820
The primary products of photosynthesis,

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:40.390
carbohydrates and sugars,

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:41.910
sustain the plant itself,

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:44.090
and the plant can then in turn

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:45.950
be consumed as part of the food chain.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.900
Yet only a few of the many chemicals

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.120
produced by plants are primary compounds.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:09.080
Another group of chemicals called secondary compounds are unusual substances that do not seem essential to the plant's basic life cycle.

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:19.160
For the most part, the functions served by these secondary compounds are still unknown.

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:29.980
Why does oleander manufacture cardioactive glycosides that can paralyze the lungs, cause bleeding, and...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:39.090
even death? What is the role of calcium oxalate in household caledians? In humans, it irritates

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:47.780
the throat and can lead to asphyxiation. And why does belladonna produce hallucinogenic compounds?

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:55.380
One view is that the plants need these substances for protection and defense.

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:59.160
It comes back to the competition.

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:05.300
Between living organisms for nutrients and for example if you look at the tropical areas

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:12.860
You have a tremendous variety of plants and all of them have everything they need to grow very very prolifically

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:22.300
They have oxygen they have light they have soil warm temperature everything except space and the limitations of living space

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:29.730
Force these plants to prepare and to secrete different sorts of

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:39.480
which enhance their ability to survive either by preventing predation or preventing attack by insects

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:47.220
or by enhancing the destruction or limiting the ability of other plants to grow around them.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:54.220
But scientists are not in complete agreement about the role of secondary compounds.

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:57.730
For some, they remain mysterious entities.

00:10:57.730 --> 00:10:59.970
We don't...

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.450
know what their function is in plants we have absolutely no idea and a lot of people are

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:11.430
inclined to dismiss this as being idle metabolic chatter on the part of the plant for example

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:16.770
some useless chemical the plant doesn't know what it's doing so it spends a lot of its time

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:22.900
dreaming up some scheme for making weird molecules which people like myself might be

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:29.970
interested in you know whatever purpose they may serve in the plant these second

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.330
secondary compounds are the critical components of plant medicines for human use. And the search

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:45.110
for hidden power in plants has often revolutionized medicine. In 1803, a young German pharmacist,

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:49.710
Friedrichsher Turner, was looking for the active substance contained in opium from the poppy,

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:59.600
whose good and evil influence had been known for centuries. He was the first to identify an alkaloid,

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.560
secondary plant product characterized by its molecular structure, which includes at least

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:14.400
one nitrogen atom, and by its ability to affect the human body. Once the alkaloid had been isolated,

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:19.750
Sir Turner could refine the opium into a more powerful drug with more precise action in the

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:25.940
body. After testing the new drug on himself and his colleagues, he named it morphine,

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:29.960
for Morpheus, the Greek god of sleep, and its pain-killing

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:38.680
properties remain unsurpassed alkaloids are the active compounds in such familiar drugs as nicotine

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:50.630
caffeine and cocaine they have also accounted for modern-day medical breakthroughs the madagascar

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:55.930
periwinkle has a long history as a traditional remedy used to treat everything from sore throats

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:59.960
to diabetes in the night

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:10.240
In the 1950s, scientists discovered that an alkaloid called vincristine, produced by the periwinkle, had a new application in treating childhood leukemia and other cancers.

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:18.680
Ten-month-old Laura is receiving vincristine as part of a program of therapy to stop the growth of a malignant tumor.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:23.700
Her physician, Dr. Mark Greenberg, describes how vincristine works.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:28.630
Vincristine is a mitotic spindle poison.

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:29.980
What the drug actually does...

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:38.140
is to arrest the division of cells. When a cell of any sort divides its

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:42.600
chromosomes split into two and then attach themselves to what's called a

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:47.000
mitotic spindle. The mitotic spindle helps to pull the two halves of the

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:51.600
chromosomes apart. What Vincristian actually does biologically is to poison

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:57.750
that spindle or the tubules that form that spindle. It does this preferentially

00:13:57.750 --> 00:13:59.180
with malignant cells.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:07.070
is really attaching to any tubules, any mitotic tubules, in malignant cells that are in the process of dividing.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:14.340
The use of vincristine is just one part of an aggressive medical approach to childhood cancer.

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:18.660
We almost never use drugs by themselves.

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:23.440
We use a combination of drugs that act at various points in the cell cycle.

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:29.100
So vincristine used in combination with many other drugs is enormously effective in this sort of range.

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:33.790
malignant disease in childhood. Between the new drugs and the

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:37.520
the new methods of chemotherapy we've gone from what was

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:42.480
perhaps 10 or 15 percent five-year survival in childhood leukemias

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:51.060
now up above 85 or 90 percent. Other life-saving medical breakthroughs have

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:52.840
recently come from the natural world.

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:57.650
A vacation stroll in Norway 15 years ago

00:14:57.700 --> 00:14:59.960
led to a discovery that revolutionized

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.120
the science of organ transplantation.

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:07.810
An employee of a Swiss drug company

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:08.670
looking for dirt

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.610
collected a few spoonfuls of mud

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:11.610
from a murky swamp.

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:17.560
He brought the mud back to the lab

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:18.980
here in Basel, Switzerland.

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:24.090
Because many antibiotics

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:25.510
have been discovered in soil,

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:28.070
drug companies routinely check soil samples

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:29.230
from all over the world

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:29.960
to see what...

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:40.040
they contain. Among the several microbes which grew from the Norwegian mud was a fungus that

00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:51.460
had never been seen before. This new fungus produced a chemical that had an unexpected benefit.

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:59.910
It could counteract organ rejection. Although the surgical tech

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:04.580
that allowed transplants have been in use since the first kidney transplant in 1954.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:08.240
The transplants themselves were only moderately successful.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:16.100
In order for transplants to work, a significant problem must be overcome.

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:23.660
Transplanted organ is perceived as a foreign invader by the body's white blood cells,

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:27.040
which then attack the new organ, in this case a kidney.

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:29.960
This immune response...

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.730
overwhelms the organ and leads to rejection, unless the recipient is given

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:41.960
immunosuppressant drugs. These drugs kill the white cells that cause rejection, but

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:46.230
they also kill all the other cells that protect the body from intruders, leaving

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:54.390
the patient vulnerable to serious infection. More than ever before,

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:59.520
cyclosporine solved the problem when the white blood cells start to attack a new

00:16:59.520 --> 00:16:59.840
organ.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:08.890
cyclosporine calls them off but unlike the other anti-rejection drugs it doesn't kill them it just

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:15.410
stops their assault so the organ is protected but the white blood cells can still patrol the body

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:24.490
and keep it free from infection over 7 000 transplants are performed in the u.s each year

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:29.910
since cyclosporine was first made available in 1983 the success rate of certain

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:38.500
transplants has grown to nearly 80 percent. The recent discovery of such drugs as cyclosporine

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:43.600
and vincristine suggests that there is still untapped potential in nature's chemistry and

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:50.190
has inspired the search for new medical products from plants. When you consider that all the drugs

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:56.920
that come from plants in the world come from about 90 species and there are 250,000 at least

00:17:56.920 --> 00:17:59.380
species in the world, then

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:09.680
Common sense tells you there have to be more drugs in plants if we rely on only 90 of them to provide 25% of the medication in the United States.

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:21.710
Dr. Richard Schultes of Harvard University has spent more than 40 years searching for plants that might help realize this potential.

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:29.520
There are a half a million species of plants in the world. My interest is purely medicinal.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:39.400
But I hope one day at least one of the plants that I have brought to the attention of science may give us a new medicine.

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:47.020
An ethnobotanist, Schultes specializes in the medical uses of plants in traditional cultures.

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:55.340
His work has focused on the rainforests of the Amazon and in teaching others the ways of the jungle.

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:59.790
The blowgun among the peoples who use it is...

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:06.400
Blow guns and poison arrows have played a significant role in Schulte's research,

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:09.240
and he enjoys showing off his prowess with them.

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:16.800
Of course, I'm in a building here, and I can't shoot very high.

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:21.990
But we're going to try to see if we can hit that target up there.

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:43.940
When I got my Ph.D. at Harvard in 1941, I had a grant to go down to work on arrow poisons in the headwaters of the Amazon.

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:51.570
Arrow poisons, which the natives used to kill animals, were becoming very important in medicine.

00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:59.080
The best known of those arrow poisons, curare, has become indispensable as a muscle relaxant during surgery.

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:04.080
To Schultes, the Amazon was an obvious starting place for botanical research.

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:08.560
When you look at any flora, even our own...

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:10.890
depauperate flora here in New England.

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:11.820
and Canada.

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:14.830
It's a...

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:15.500
veritable

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:17.030
chemical factory.

00:20:18.490 --> 00:20:20.570
Multiply our 1,800.

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:21.650
1900.

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:24.380
Species. Multiply that.

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:26.800
to 80,000 and you can see

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:29.040
What an emporium.

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:31.240
of chemical wealth.

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:32.920
The Amazon is.

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:38.620
Schultes himself has collected over 24,000 plant specimens.

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:40.600
many of them previously unknown.

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:44.060
With such plant riches available for study,

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:49.500
botanists must find a way to focus their research on the plants most likely to have medicinal value.

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:51.500
If

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:52.490
chemists.

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:59.590
are going to have to get material of and analyze 80,000 species, they'll never finish the job.

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:01.680
One short cut.

00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:03.020
is to

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:07.050
concentrate on those plants that the natives have found.

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:08.950
to have some activity.

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:10.650
on the human body.

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:17.880
Schultes found the local medicine men to be his most valuable tutors in plant medicines.

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:20.340
He even participated in their rituals.

00:21:21.270 --> 00:21:22.690
He's the tall one on the right.

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:29.980
Mark Plotkin, an ethnobotanist who was trained by Schultes, also works in the...

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:30.420
Amazon.

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:31.910
using the same methods.

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:39.060
Well, according to Professor Schultes, the oldest profession in the world is actually that of the shaman or witch doctor.

00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:42.110
through the course of human history, most of

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:44.420
The world's medicines have come from plants.

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:47.160
It's only relatively recently with the advent of modern technology

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:49.820
that we've been able to reduce our dependence on.

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:52.150
medicinal plants as a source of medicines.

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:57.480
The medicine man or shaman or witch doctor or brujo or curandero is the person.

00:21:57.900 --> 00:21:59.940
who knows the most about these plants.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:09.490
Now, if you look in Western literature and movies and films and books, he's often been derided as somebody who runs around with rubber snakes screaming gibberish and mumbo-jumbo.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:14.870
But the people who've had the real pleasure and the opportunity to work with these people have found out quite the opposite.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:16.960
that this is a very serious profession.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:19.640
This is the predecessor of modern doctors,

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:22.800
and we have a lot to learn from these people.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:29.520
This is Dionisio Aguinda.

00:22:30.860 --> 00:22:33.840
He is a brujol or medicine man of the Ecuadoran Amazon.

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:46.000
He is searching for plant remedies he can use in treating his patients.

00:22:50.060 --> 00:22:50.560
for a guinda.

00:22:50.830 --> 00:22:51.750
A Kichwa Indian.

00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:54.090
The rainforest is a living pharmacy.

00:22:55.140 --> 00:22:55.660
A potent.

00:22:56.010 --> 00:22:58.990
varied and infinitely renewable source of plant medicines.

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:19.460
Guinda uses this bark as a treatment for pain.

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:29.170
He draws on an extensive but unwritten body of knowledge about plant medicines and their preparation.

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:36.320
For today's treatment, the bark is prepared according to a formula that calls for it to be boiled.

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:38.170
along with several other ingredients.

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:41.110
the formula, along with countless others.

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:43.940
was passed down to him from his father and grandfather.

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:45.600
who were also medicine men.

00:23:50.180 --> 00:23:53.640
His patients often journey for days through the jungle to seek his help.

00:23:56.390 --> 00:23:58.450
This woman has a persistent stomach pain.

00:24:02.780 --> 00:24:07.120
