purpose by different peoples where we're going to find new and important medicines for the future.

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:45.380
This remote part of Ecuador has recently become the focus of intensive scientific study,

00:26:46.160 --> 00:26:51.060
part of a systematic effort to document the elaborate medical tradition of the Quichua Indians.

00:26:55.690 --> 00:26:59.970
In a collaborative venture, ethnobotanist Robin Marles of the University of...

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:05.230
Illinois, and David Neal of the Missouri Botanical Gardens head off on a collecting trip.

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:14.200
Our project is involved in basic biological inventory of the Amazon forest to find out

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:20.190
just what's here, what plants are here, because although this region, the Upper Napa, is

00:27:20.190 --> 00:27:24.640
one of the richest areas biologically in the world, it's very poorly known.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:35.440
Aguinda allows the scientists to accompany him as he sets out on one of his routine trips to collect

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:49.460
medicinal plants. This fieldwork is part of a massive effort to identify and investigate nearly

00:27:49.460 --> 00:27:52.780
2,000 therapeutic plants used in the Northwest Amazon.

00:28:01.630 --> 00:28:09.330
Even to experienced naturalists, the sheer diversity of the Amazon, with its more than 80,000 plant species, is overwhelming.

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:16.460
Without Aguinda's guidance, their chances of discovering useful medicinal plants are remote.

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:35.900
Aguinda's intimate knowledge of the jungle and its healing plants has been a family legacy for generations.

00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:42.310
But around the world, the knowledge of such natural healers is on the verge of disappearing.

00:28:44.450 --> 00:28:48.010
Aguinda's son, Enrico, represents the end of the family tradition.

00:28:49.630 --> 00:28:54.640
Instead of training as a medicine man like his father, Enrico has become a guide and translator

00:28:54.640 --> 00:28:59.980
for the growing traffic of prospectors, oil men, and even tourists who now filter into the...

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:09.850
heart of the forest when Aguinda dies his family legacy will pass from the human record and science

00:29:09.850 --> 00:29:16.410
will lose a vital link to the mysterious plant kingdom what we're looking at is a culture

00:29:16.410 --> 00:29:20.860
that's standing on the edge of a precipice and this culture and this knowledge is going to

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:25.480
disappear over the edge of that precipice unless we work to document that knowledge in the next

00:29:25.480 --> 00:29:29.870
generation every time one of these old medicine men dies it's as if

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:35.080
library has burned down. In fact, it's worse than that. If a library in this country burns down,

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:40.010
most of that knowledge is found in other libraries as well. When these old medicine men die,

00:29:40.360 --> 00:29:50.080
that knowledge is lost forever. With an increasing sense of urgency, the research team will attempt to

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:58.380
make a permanent record of Aginda's medical knowledge. Following his leads, they continue

00:29:58.380 --> 00:29:59.960
their search for samples of the...

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:10.730
Seguinda uses which might have further medical potential. This field work in Ecuador resulted

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:18.390
in the collection of over 200 species. Before the scientists leave the jungle they must prepare

00:30:18.390 --> 00:30:29.860
the plant specimens to be shipped back to the U.S. Through the ages botanists have used similar

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:36.790
methods to organize and preserve their findings. Samples of each plant called

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:40.610
voucher specimens are first sorted according to common characteristics

00:30:40.610 --> 00:30:44.340
such as leaf shape and structure or presence of flowers.

00:30:46.700 --> 00:30:50.180
The voucher specimens are then pressed between layers of newspaper to dry.

00:30:55.060 --> 00:30:59.300
Known species are identified on site. For other species, any special

00:30:59.300 --> 00:30:59.800
characteristics

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:02.340
that might aid in identification are noted.

00:31:04.680 --> 00:31:08.240
Unfamiliar specimens may also be recorded by a botanical illustrator.

00:31:12.800 --> 00:31:15.940
In some cases, a plant will warrant more than a single specimen.

00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:18.820
If it seems particularly interesting,

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:24.140
for example, something that's used to treat intestinal parasites

00:31:24.140 --> 00:31:29.970
or something that's used to treat sores which don't heal for a long time,

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:37.380
time, then what I would like to do is to collect about five kilos dry weight of the plant to

00:31:37.850 --> 00:31:47.200
bring back to Chicago for chemical and biological analysis. Plant chemist Dr. Bryce Douglas is

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:52.500
familiar with the obstacles faced by collectors when large amounts of plant materials are required.

00:31:53.900 --> 00:31:59.970
That is when it is extremely difficult, keeping in mind that very often the tropical conditions are

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:06.100
90 degrees Fahrenheit and a hundred percent humidity try to dry natural

00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:10.160
materials try to get the water out of them under those conditions it's very

00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:14.600
difficult and if you don't be simply rot so there are a lot of logistics of that

00:32:14.600 --> 00:32:20.810
sort that have made the chances of success just a little bit tougher once

00:32:24.580 --> 00:32:28.570
the samples are back in the lab in Chicago Robin Marls can begin the

00:32:28.570 --> 00:32:29.790
detailed chemical analysis

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:31.780
of the team's collected specimens.

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:35.220
It may be months or even years

00:32:35.220 --> 00:32:37.080
before they know if they've discovered anything.

00:32:38.990 --> 00:32:41.470
You're only going to find activity in a few percent

00:32:41.650 --> 00:32:44.210
of all the plant extracts that you test.

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:48.020
Then from there, you've only got the initial stage,

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:51.890
which is some sort of a crude extract or gum or goo

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:54.070
that has the biological activity you're after.

00:32:54.620 --> 00:32:56.600
You've then got to go into the chemical side

00:32:56.840 --> 00:32:59.750
and start purifying that.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:05.660
you can get it down to a pure compound that takes an awful lot of time and work and at each stage

00:33:05.660 --> 00:33:12.860
you've got to follow it by biological assays looking for the same activity that's desired in

00:33:12.860 --> 00:33:21.880
the end so as to make sure that you're on the track of the active substance one active substance

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:27.630
of potential pharmacological value has recently been detected at the university of british columbia

00:33:28.550 --> 00:33:29.970
Here, scientists have found...

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:38.360
unusual and highly toxic chemicals in a variety of garden marigold. The marigolds contain compounds

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:44.310
called polyacetylenes, which seem to be showing some promise in lab experiments as anti-tumor

00:33:44.310 --> 00:33:52.590
agents. In this demonstration, an extract from the marigold is added in equal parts to two dishes

00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:59.580
containing mosquito larvae. The larvae absorb the polyacetylene molecules into their bodies.

00:34:03.680 --> 00:34:06.660
These molecules are unusual because they are photosensitive,

00:34:07.340 --> 00:34:09.560
becoming active only in the presence of sunlight.

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:14.740
The specimens are placed in an ultraviolet light chamber.

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:18.390
One dish is covered so that its contents are shielded.

00:34:20.890 --> 00:34:23.670
Combination of polyacetylenes and ultraviolet light

00:34:23.790 --> 00:34:28.470
causes a violent reaction that destroys the cells of larvae in the unshielded dish.

00:34:29.480 --> 00:34:29.960
Those in the...

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:38.730
covered dish remain unharmed. Researchers are hopeful that such light-activated toxins could

00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:43.890
be modified for use in the human body, where they would be targeted to destroy particular cancer

00:34:43.890 --> 00:34:51.910
cells while leaving the body's normal cells intact. Extensive research into anti-cancer drugs

00:34:51.910 --> 00:34:56.550
that could be derived from plants is now being sponsored by the National Cancer Institute.

00:34:57.720 --> 00:34:59.980
It's the largest program of its kind in the U.S.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:12.570
We've screened something over 35,000 plants, different plants, different species, over 120,000 extracts.

00:35:12.820 --> 00:35:20.780
And when you're looking at a large variety of substances with biological activities, you're going to find some that have good medicinal activity.

00:35:24.070 --> 00:35:29.750
Thanks to the development of new technologies, the NCI scientists have become increasingly sophisticated.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:33.540
in their approach to screening for anti-cancer agents in plants.

